data/*
!data/projection_2d.json
!data/projection_2d_ability.json
!data/combo_graph.json
!data/combo_details.json
!data/card_roles.json
!data/embeddings.bin
!data/embeddings_ability.bin
!data/synergy_graph.json
!data/obsolescence_index.json
!data/regions_default.json
!data/regions_ability.json
# The discovery front door: viz_index.json + neighbours.bin are what let the browser
# land on a card and branch from it without the 12.9 MB projection or the 16.8 MB
# embedding matrix. Served by Pages, so they must be tracked like every other viz
# artifact — and they belong to the index invariant, so they regenerate with the
# pipeline, never on their own.
!data/viz_index.json
!data/neighbours.bin
# CardBERT's viz artifacts. Tracked for the same reason the function space's are:
# GitHub Pages serves this directory as static files, there is no LFS (Pages would
# serve the pointer), and a browser cannot run the pipeline. Without these four the
# similarity toggle 404s in production while working locally — the worst kind of
# difference between the two. ~34.8 MB, doubling the tracked embedding set.
!data/embeddings_cardbert.bin
!data/neighbours_cardbert.bin
!data/projection_2d_cardbert.json
!data/regions_cardbert.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.DS_Store
.claude/settings.local.json
!data/decks/
# The pod: opponent seats for `simulate --vs` (docs/simulation.md S3) — tracked, like decks.
!data/opponents/
# THE NAMED TABLES. Tracked for the same reason `data/opponents/` is: a
# pod is a claim about which table a result was measured against, so a
# fresh clone that cannot resolve `--pod standard` cannot reproduce a
# single record. `data/*` catches it otherwise, which is how the first
# cut of this would have passed locally and failed on CI.
!data/pods/
!data/strategy/
# A physical card collection (`COLLECTION_DIR`), read by `deck-history` to tell a
# proposed swap you already own from one you would have to buy. Tracked so a
# clone has a worked example; override with MANAMAP_COLLECTION_DIR to use yours
# rather than inheriting someone else's boxes.
!data/collection/
# The similarity golden set is hand-authored, not generated — losing it means losing
# the only measurement that can tell a good embedding from a collapsed one.
!data/eval/
data/strategy/strategy_index.json
data/strategy/strategy_embeddings.npy
data/strategy/.strategy-db-meta.json

# Agent scratchpad: subagents write their JSON here and return only the path, so a
# 133 KB candidate_pool never crosses the orchestrator's context. The orchestrator
# validates and merges into the tracked artifact; these are working files.
data/decks/*/.agent-out/
# Forge simulation: the raw game logs are samples, large, and regenerable; the run
# record beside them (data/decks/<slug>/sim/<run-id>.json) is tracked.
data/decks/*/sim/logs/
# THE SAME RULE, ONE LEVEL DOWN. The pattern above does not match
# `data/decks/<slug>/branches/<name>/sim/logs/`, so a branch's logs were TRACKED
# while the deck's own were ignored — 12 MB of them, committed the day branches
# shipped. A path rule written before a directory level existed does not learn
# about it, and nothing complains: the files are valid, just enormous.
data/decks/*/branches/*/sim/logs/
data/decks/*/branches/*/sim/scenarios/
# sim-scenario drafts: a lifted board with no question yet; `--stack` promotes a posed one
# into stacks/ (tracked).
data/decks/*/sim/scenarios/
# experiment logs: regenerable exactly — each arm's decklist text is inside the tracked artifact
data/decks/*/experiments/logs/

# `data/decks/*/versions.json` USED TO BE IGNORED, and the argument was half
# right. A version row carries the sha and date of the commit that created it,
# and those are unknowable inside that commit — so a copy written in the SAME
# commit as a decklist change is one version behind.
#
# What the argument missed: (1) nothing anywhere reads `sha`, `first_sha` or
# `subject` — the rap sheet reads version, date, in[], out[], record and tags,
# all derivable from content or from PAST commits; (2) the tracked
# `deck_versions.json` already stores `paper.sha` and `tags[…].sha`, commit shas
# written in a LATER commit than the one they name. The constraint is a workflow
# rule the repo already follows, not a physical one.
#
# And the "deploy-time step" this rule deferred to was never built, so the file
# 404s in production and the deck page's rap sheet — the spine of the dossier —
# renders its empty state to every reader. That is the exact failure the
# CardBERT allowlist above exists to prevent: works locally, 404s deployed.
#
# It is TRACKED now, regenerated by `make manuals`, and gated by
# `test_versions_json_matches_a_fresh_run`. One rule: a commit may not contain
# both a decklist and its versions.json — see tests/test_pilot_commit_protocol.py.

# A POOL IS SCRATCH. `pool.txt` is "consider these" — written by the Atlas's
# `pool/save` AND by `close --write`, read by `assess` and
# `candidates --pool library`, and re-derivable in one command. It was TRACKED
# with two writers and no gate, which is the one wrong option: a committed file
# nothing validates and either surface can silently overwrite.
data/decks/*/pool.txt

# `check-in` copies the old list aside before overwriting it — a local safety
# net, not an artifact. Untracked and un-ignored, it rode into a commit on a
# `git add -A` and became a tracked stray the docs gate then flagged. The gate
# was right; the file should never have been committable.
data/decks/*/decklist.txt.bak
